(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-valvular-heart-disease-quiz"]={"title":"Valvular Heart Disease Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"How does chronic mitral regurgitation progress?","choices":["Annular dilation, tricuspid leak, then isolated right failure","Left heart failure, pulmonary hypertension, then both sides fail","Left atrial clot, stroke, then sudden cardiac death","Concentric hypertrophy, stiffness, then preserved-fraction failure"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Chronic mitral regurgitation dilates the left ventricle with eccentric hypertrophy, causing left heart failure, then pulmonary hypertension, and finally combined right and left heart failure. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 46, 47"},{"q":"When should pulmonic stenosis be referred to cardiology?","choices":["Only if it is part of tetralogy of Fallot","Only after a 20-year follow-up","Whenever it is seen on echocardiogram","Only once right heart failure appears"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Whether congenital or related to heart failure, pulmonic stenosis should be referred to a cardiologist whenever it is present on an echocardiogram. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"How is pulmonic regurgitation usually discovered?","choices":["Incidentally, on echo or examination","By catheterization after syncope","By an electrocardiogram showing right axis deviation","By a chest radiograph after hemoptysis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonic regurgitation is usually an incidental finding on an echocardiogram or on physical examination; management is to treat the underlying condition. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 64"},{"q":"What should a patient receiving a mechanical heart valve be told about anticoagulation?","choices":["It is only for surgery","It will be needed for life","It stops after six weeks","Aspirin alone will do"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A mechanical valve needs lifelong anticoagulation with a vitamin K antagonist, most commonly warfarin, monitored against an international normalized ratio target. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 21, 75"},{"q":"How long after rheumatic fever is rheumatic mitral stenosis usually diagnosed?","choices":["About 5 years","About 50 years","About 20 years","Within 1 year"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Mitral stenosis from rheumatic fever is usually diagnosed about 20 years after the fever, and symptoms stay rare until the orifice has narrowed to about a third of normal. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 34"},{"q":"Rheumatic heart disease is an autoimmune reaction to which infection?","choices":["Viridans group streptococcus","Group A beta-hemolytic streptococcus","Group B beta-hemolytic streptococcus","Staphylococcus aureus bacteremia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rheumatic heart disease is an autoimmune reaction to group A beta-hemolytic streptococci. It is an acquired cause of aortic stenosis and the most common cause of mitral stenosis. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"What therapy does asymptomatic mitral stenosis in sinus rhythm need?","choices":["A loop diuretic","Balloon valvotomy","Valve replacement","No therapy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Asymptomatic mitral stenosis with sinus rhythm needs no therapy. Loop diuretics are added once dyspnea and orthopnea appear, and intervention waits for severe disease with symptoms. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 41"},{"q":"Which study diagnoses tricuspid regurgitation?","choices":["Doppler echocardiogram","Left heart catheterization","Chest radiograph","Electrocardiogram"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Doppler echocardiography shows the regurgitation and the leaflets, which may be prolapsed, scarred or displaced. Referral follows when it is identified on echo or with right heart failure symptoms. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Which echocardiogram finding diagnoses mitral valve prolapse?","choices":["Thickened, redundant leaflets over 5 mm","Thickened, immobile leaflets, narrow orifice","Calcified aortic leaflets, poor opening","A dilated aortic root over 4 cm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The echocardiogram shows thickened, redundant mitral leaflets over 5 mm that flare into the left atrium during systole. Echocardiography is the diagnostic test. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"What is the most common cause of aortic stenosis in patients older than 70?","choices":["Infective endocarditis","Degenerative (atherosclerotic) disease","A congenital bicuspid aortic valve","Rheumatic heart disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Degenerative heart disease, the atherosclerotic process driven by hyperlipidemia, diabetes and smoking, is the most common cause of aortic stenosis over 70. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 12"},{"q":"Which neck findings fit tricuspid regurgitation?","choices":["A collapsing pulse with head bobbing","Hoarseness from nerve compression","Raised jugular pressure and a venous thrill","A weak, delayed carotid pulse"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation raises the jugular venous pressure with neck pulsations and a venous thrill over the right jugular vein, as blood is driven back into the right atrium. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which long-term anticoagulant class is recommended for a mechanical heart valve?","choices":["A vitamin K antagonist","An antiplatelet agent","Heparin alone","No anticoagulant"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. For patients with mechanical heart valves, vitamin K antagonist therapy is recommended over no vitamin K antagonist and over antiplatelet agents for long-term management. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 75"},{"q":"Which patient is preferred for a bioprosthetic rather than a mechanical valve?","choices":["One with mild, asymptomatic valve disease","One with a contraindication to anticoagulation","One whose life expectancy exceeds the valve's","One who wants lifelong vitamin K antagonist therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bioprosthetic valves are preferred when there is any contraindication to anticoagulation, or when life expectancy is shorter than the expected longevity of the bioprosthetic valve. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which procedure treats severe, symptomatic pulmonic stenosis?","choices":["Balloon valvuloplasty","A mitral valve clip","Tricuspid annuloplasty","Afterload reduction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severe pulmonic stenosis with symptoms is treated with diuretics for right heart failure symptoms and pulmonic balloon valvuloplasty; severe congenital disease may need replacement. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 59"},{"q":"What can mitral valve prolapse progress to?","choices":["Tricuspid stenosis","Mitral regurgitation","Mitral stenosis","Aortic stenosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Mitral valve prolapse is usually benign and asymptomatic but may develop the symptoms and signs of mitral regurgitation, at which point clip, transcatheter replacement or valve replacement are options. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 54"},{"q":"In the United States, what is tricuspid stenosis almost always?","choices":["Congenital","Rheumatic","Iatrogenic","Degenerative"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In the United States tricuspid stenosis is almost always congenital, while worldwide it is most commonly due to rheumatic fever. It is most common in women. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 65"},{"q":"Which patients with aortic stenosis on echocardiogram should have a cardiology consult?","choices":["Only severe stenosis","Only those older than 70","All of them","Only symptomatic patients"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Every patient with evidence of aortic stenosis on an echocardiogram should have a cardiology consult, to evaluate the valve and decide how often it is followed. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which is the high-pressure cause of pulmonic regurgitation?","choices":["Carcinoid disease","Surgical valvotomy","A bicuspid valve","Pulmonary hypertension"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Pulmonary hypertension is the high-pressure cause of pulmonic regurgitation. The low-pressure causes are a congenital bicuspid valve, carcinoid disease and iatrogenic injury, the most common cause overall. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 61"},{"q":"Which echocardiogram values define severe mitral stenosis?","choices":["Area <1.0 cm², mean gradient >10 mmHg","Area <1.0 cm², mean gradient >40 mmHg","Area <2.5 cm², mean gradient >25 mmHg","Area <3.0 cm², mean gradient >60 mmHg"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Severe mitral stenosis is a valve area under 1.0 cm² with a mean gradient over 10 mmHg and a peak velocity over 3.0 m/s, with proximal flow always present. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 40"},{"q":"Which is an acute cause of mitral regurgitation?","choices":["Mitral valve prolapse","Dilated cardiomyopathy","Hypertrophic cardiomyopathy","Papillary muscle rupture"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Acute mitral regurgitation follows acute myocardial infarction with papillary muscle rupture, chordae tendineae rupture, infective endocarditis, rheumatic fever, acute left ventricular dilation or prosthetic valve failure. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"What is the survival of untreated severe aortic regurgitation once the ejection fraction falls?","choices":["About 10 to 15 years","About 5 years","About 20 years","About 2 to 3 years"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic aortic regurgitation with mildly reduced or reduced ejection fraction, symptomatic or not, is a surgical candidate; untreated, survival is only about 2 to 3 years. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 26, 31"},{"q":"How does chronic aortic regurgitation remodel the left ventricle?","choices":["Concentric hypertrophy, small cavity","Right ventricular pressure overload","No change until the valve calcifies","Eccentric hypertrophy and dilation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Blood returns to the left ventricle in diastole, the ventricle compensates with eccentric hypertrophy and dilation, and the endpoint is systolic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 25"},{"q":"Which maneuver makes the mitral regurgitation murmur softer?","choices":["Standing","Squatting","Handgrip","Expiration"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The mitral regurgitation murmur decreases with standing or the Valsalva maneuver. It increases with squatting and handgrip, which raise resistance to ejection and push more blood back into the left atrium. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 48"},{"q":"Which aortic stenosis procedure is used in children and young adults?","choices":["Transcatheter aortic valve replacement","Mechanical aortic valve replacement","Tricuspid annuloplasty","Percutaneous aortic balloon valvuloplasty"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In children and young adults, aortic stenosis is treated with percutaneous aortic balloon valvuloplasty. Older adults with severe symptomatic disease receive valve replacement, such as transcatheter replacement. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 21"},{"q":"Which pairing describes a regurgitant (leaking) valve?","choices":["Rumbling murmur and volume overload","Blowing murmur and pressure overload","Blowing murmur and volume overload","Harsh murmur and pressure overload"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Regurgitation is abnormal backflow through a valve that does not close completely. The murmur is described as blowing, and the extra blood returning each beat produces volume overload. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 8, 73"},{"q":"Which finding distinguishes tricuspid regurgitation from mitral regurgitation?","choices":["The murmur is holosystolic","The murmur is blowing","The murmur softens on standing","It grows louder on inspiration"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Both are holosystolic, but the tricuspid regurgitation murmur increases with inspiration, the Carvallo sign, because inspiration raises right-sided venous return. That distinguishes it from mitral regurgitation. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"Which medical measures treat severe, symptomatic tricuspid stenosis?","choices":["Hydralazine, nitroprusside, intravenous fluids","Warfarin, heparin bridging, antiplatelets","Low salt, diuretics, an aldosterone antagonist","Nitrates, beta blockers, calcium channel blockers"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Severe symptomatic tricuspid stenosis is managed with a low-salt diet, diuretics and an aldosterone antagonist, with bioprosthetic valve replacement preferred when surgery is needed. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 69"},{"q":"Where is the tricuspid regurgitation murmur heard?","choices":["Right second space, radiating to the carotids","Left second space, toward the right sternum","Lower left sternal border, near the epigastrium","At the apex, radiating to the axilla"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Tricuspid regurgitation is a holosystolic murmur at the left mid or lower sternal border near the epigastrium that increases with inspiration (the Carvallo sign). — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 71"},{"q":"How do mechanical and biological valves differ in anticoagulation?","choices":["Neither needs anticoagulation","Mechanical needs it lifelong; biological does not","Biological needs it lifelong; mechanical does not","Both need lifelong anticoagulation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A mechanical valve (titanium and carbon) needs lifelong anticoagulation, while a biological valve (pig or cow tissue) does not need anticoagulation beyond the immediate postoperative period. — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slides 21, 52, 74"},{"q":"How is the mitral stenosis murmur best heard?","choices":["With the diaphragm, patient squatting","With the bell, patient in left lateral decubitus","With the diaphragm, patient sitting forward","With the bell, patient standing upright"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The low-pitched rumble of mitral stenosis is heard best with the bell at the apex, with the patient in the left lateral decubitus position (lying on the left side). — Valvular Heart Disease - Carter.pptx, Slide 38"}]};
